\title{The Fractal Dimension of Proof Search:\\
Similarity Dimension, Relative Entropy, and Subadditive Growth}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-10}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a rigorous model of the difficulty of automated proof search based on the
geometry of the tree of derivations. A derivation problem is modeled as a complete
$b$-ary tree of candidate partial proofs, together with a \emph{self-similar} success
structure in which exactly $s$ of the $b$ available inference steps at each node can
be extended to a completed proof. The set of infinite successful derivation paths,
under the natural ultrametric on the boundary of the tree, is a self-similar Cantor
set whose similarity dimension we call the \emph{proof-search fractal dimension}
$D(b,s) = \log s / \log b$. We prove that this exponent is an exact analytic
invariant: the number of successful depth-$n$ paths equals the total candidate count
raised to $D$ (the \emph{Bridge Identity} $s^n = (b^n)^D$), the success density decays
as $(b^n)^{D-1}$ (the \emph{Density Law}), and $D \in [0,1]$ with $D = 1$ a sharp
threshold attained only when no branch can be pruned. We reinterpret $D$ as a
\emph{relative entropy}: the per-depth growth rate of successful paths, $\log s$,
divided by the ambient growth rate $\log b$, connecting the model to Fekete's theory
of subadditive sequences. We show hardness is governed by the \emph{codimension}
$\kappa = 1 - D$, the exponential rate at which successful paths thin out, correcting
the misconception that hard theorems have dimension exceeding $1$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Automated theorem proving is, at its core, a search problem. A prover explores a tree
of partial derivations: the root is the goal, the children of a node are the results
of applying each applicable inference rule, and a proof is a path descending through
the tree. The unifying question is how the \emph{difficulty} of a problem is
reflected in the structure of this tree. Coarse answers---worst-case exponential
blowup, undecidability of validity in general---capture the pessimistic extremes but
say nothing quantitative about a particular problem.

We propose a finer invariant drawn from fractal geometry. A search tree with a
self-similar success structure has, on its boundary, a self-similar Cantor set of
successful paths, and self-similar sets carry a well-defined dimension. This
dimension controls, exactly, the growth of successful path counts, the density of
success among candidates, and the entropy of the search. Our contributions are:
(1) a precise model of self-similar proof search with fractal dimension
$D(b,s) = \log s / \log b$; (2) the Bridge Identity $s^n = (b^n)^D$; (3) the Density
Law $(s/b)^n = (b^n)^{D-1}$ and the codimension $\kappa = 1 - D$ as the pruning rate;
(4) sharp structural facts, $D \in [0,1]$, strict monotonicity, and $D = 1 \iff s =
b$; (5) the reinterpretation of $D$ as a relative entropy and its connection to
Fekete's subadditive theory; and (6) the exact cost of exhaustive search.

\section{The model}

Fix a branching factor $b \in \N$ with $b > 1$. The \emph{candidate search space} of
depth $n$ is the complete $b$-ary tree; its paths of length $n$ number
$\total(b, n) = b^n$. A derivation problem is \emph{self-similar with success factor}
$s$, where $1 \le s \le b$, if at every node exactly $s$ of the $b$ available
inference steps extend to a completed proof. Then the \emph{successful} paths of
length $n$ number $\suc(s, n) = s^n$. The condition $s \ge 1$ encodes solvability;
$b > 1$ encodes genuine branching.

Let $\partial T_b$ be the set of infinite paths of the tree. For $x, y \in \partial
T_b$ let $|x \wedge y|$ denote the length of their longest common prefix; the natural
ultrametric is $d(x, y) = b^{-|x \wedge y|}$. Under $d$, $\partial T_b$ is a compact
self-similar set of similarity dimension $1$ (the attractor of $b$ contractions of
ratio $1/b$), and the set $S$ of infinite successful paths is the attractor of $s$
such contractions---a self-similar Cantor set.

\begin{definition}[Proof-search fractal dimension]
For $b > 1$ and $1 \le s \le b$, the proof-search fractal dimension is the similarity
dimension of $S$, the unique exponent $D$ solving the Moran equation
$s \cdot (1/b)^D = 1$:
\[
D(b, s) = \frac{\log s}{\log b}.
\]
Under the open set condition this equals the Hausdorff dimension of $S$.
\end{definition}

\section{Elementary counts}

\begin{proposition}[Path counts]
For all $n$, $\total(b,n) = b^n$ and $\suc(s,n) = s^n$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
A depth-$n$ path is a sequence of $n$ choices, each from $b$ (respectively $s$)
options.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Successful paths never dominate]
If $s \le b$ then $\suc(s,n) \le \total(b,n)$ for all $n$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$s \le b$ implies $s^n \le b^n$ by monotonicity of the $n$-th power.
\end{proof}

\section{The bridge identity and the density law}

\begin{theorem}[Bridge Identity]
Let $b > 1$ and $s \ge 1$. Then for every $n$,
\[
\suc(s, n) = \bigl(\total(b, n)\bigr)^{D(b,s)},
\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad
s^n = \bigl(b^n\bigr)^{\log s / \log b}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $b > 1$, $\log b > 0$, and since $s \ge 1$, $s > 0$. Then
\[
(b^n)^{\log s / \log b}
= \exp\!\left(n \log b \cdot \tfrac{\log s}{\log b}\right)
= \exp(n \log s) = s^n. \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Density Law]
Let $b > 1$ and $s \ge 1$. The fraction of candidate paths that succeed decays as a
power of the total count with exponent $D - 1$:
\[
\left(\frac{s}{b}\right)^n
= \bigl(\total(b,n)\bigr)^{D(b,s) - 1}
= \bigl(b^n\bigr)^{D - 1}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$(b^n)^{D-1} = \exp\!\big(n\log b\,(\tfrac{\log s}{\log b} - 1)\big)
= \exp\!\big(n\log(s/b)\big) = (s/b)^n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Codimension]
The pruning codimension of a self-similar search is $\kappa(b,s) = 1 - D(b,s)$.
\end{definition}

By the Density Law the success density is $(b^n)^{-\kappa}$: the codimension is the
exponential rate at which successful paths become rare. Small $\kappa$ (dimension near
$1$) means slow thinning and near-exhaustive search; large $\kappa$ means focused
search. This is the correct reading of difficulty: a self-similar subset of a
dimension-$1$ boundary can never exceed dimension $1$, so hardness is deficient
codimension, not excess dimension.

\section{The dimension lives on the balanced edge $[0,1]$}

\begin{theorem}[Range and endpoints]
Let $b > 1$ and $1 \le s \le b$. Then
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
  \item $D(b,s) \ge 0$;
  \item $D(b,s) \le 1$;
  \item $D(b,s) < 1$ whenever $s < b$;
  \item $D(b,s) = 1$ if and only if $s = b$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
(1) $\log s \ge 0$ and $\log b > 0$. (2) $s \le b$ gives $\log s \le \log b$, so
$D \le 1$. (3) $s < b$ gives $\log s < \log b$, so $D < 1$. (4) $D = 1 \iff \log s =
\log b \iff s = b$ by injectivity of $\log$ on the positive reals.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict monotonicity]
For fixed $b > 1$, the map $s \mapsto D(b,s)$ is strictly increasing on
$\{s : s \ge 1\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\log$ is strictly increasing on the positive reals, so $\log s < \log t$ for
$s < t$; dividing by $\log b > 0$ preserves the strict inequality.
\end{proof}

Thus $D = 0$ ($s = 1$) is the unique-proof endpoint; $D = 1$ ($s = b$) is the
unprunable endpoint attained only when $s = b$; intermediate values are focused but
nontrivial searches.

\section{Dimension as relative entropy and the bridge to Fekete's theory}

Define the log-count $L(n) = \log\bigl(\suc(s,n)\bigr) = \log(s^n)$.

\begin{proposition}[Exact linear growth]
$L(n) = n \log s$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Subadditivity]
$L$ is subadditive: $L(n + m) \le L(n) + L(m)$; indeed additive, so equality holds.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$L(n+m) = (n+m)\log s = n\log s + m\log s = L(n) + L(m)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Entropy limit]
$\displaystyle \lim_{n\to\infty} L(n)/n = \log s$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$L(n)/n = \log s$ for every $n \ge 1$, a constant sequence. For general subadditive
$L$, Fekete's lemma guarantees the limit exists and equals $\inf_n L(n)/n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Dimension as relative entropy]
For $b > 1$ and every depth $n \ge 1$,
\[
D(b, s) = \frac{L(n)/n}{\log b}
= \frac{\text{entropy of successful paths}}{\text{entropy of all paths}}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$L(n)/n = \log s$ for all $n \ge 1$; dividing by $\log b$ gives
$\log s / \log b = D(b,s)$.
\end{proof}

A direct consequence of subadditivity is the doubling bound $L(2n) \le 2L(n)$, tight
here because $L$ is additive. The genuine content of Fekete's theory appears for
non-uniform searches (Section~\ref{sec:ext}).

\section{Exhaustive search cost}

\begin{theorem}[Geometric cost]
An exhaustive search expanding every node down to depth $n$ visits
$\sum_{i=0}^{n} b^i$ nodes, and for $b \ge 2$,
\[
\left(\sum_{i=0}^{n} b^i\right)(b - 1) = b^{\,n+1} - 1,
\qquad
\sum_{i=0}^n b^i = \frac{b^{\,n+1}-1}{b-1}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$. For $n = 0$ the left side is $b - 1 = b^1 - 1$. For the step, add
$b^{k+1}$ and use $b^{k+1}(b-1) = b^{k+2} - b^{k+1}$ with the inductive hypothesis to
get $(b^{k+1} - 1) + (b^{k+2} - b^{k+1}) = b^{k+2} - 1$.
\end{proof}

Against this $\Theta(b^n)$ baseline, an ideal pruning searcher following only
successful branches explores $\Theta(s^n) = \Theta(b^{nD})$ paths, avoiding a
$(b^n)^{\kappa}$ factor of dead ends by the Density Law.

\section{Extensions}\label{sec:ext}

\paragraph{Variable branching.} Let $s_i$ be the success factor at depth $i$
($1 \le s_i \le b_i$, bounded). The successful-path count is $\prod_{i<n} s_i$ and
$L(n) = \sum_{i<n} \log s_i$ is genuinely subadditive. Fekete's lemma gives a search
entropy $h = \lim_n L(n)/n$, and
\[
D = \lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{\sum_{i<n}\log s_i}{\sum_{i<n}\log b_i} \in [0,1],
\]
with the Bridge Identity holding asymptotically. The dimension survives the loss of a
closed form because it is a ratio of Fekete growth rates.

\paragraph{Multifractal spectrum.} When strategies with distinct success ratios
interleave, the success set becomes multifractal: its coarse H\"older exponents fill a
nondegenerate interval and the Legendre-transform spectrum is strictly concave unless
all ratios agree. A problem is strategy-homogeneous exactly when the spectrum is a
single point.

\paragraph{Boundary cases.} If $s = 1$, $S$ is a single point of dimension $0$; if
$s = b$, $S$ is the whole boundary of dimension $1$. The case $b = 1$ is excluded:
$\log b = 0$ makes the metric degenerate.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The proof-search fractal dimension unifies three viewpoints: fractal-geometric
(similarity dimension), combinatorial (growth exponent), and information-theoretic
(relative entropy). The Bridge Identity ties them together and the Density Law
relocates difficulty to the codimension $\kappa = 1 - D$. Three directions follow:
proving the Fekete limit is well defined in $[0,1]$ for all bounded profiles;
establishing that an ideal pruning search expands $\Theta(b^{nD})$ nodes so that
proof length correlates with $1/\kappa$; and characterizing strategy-homogeneous
problems by a degenerate dimension spectrum.

\section{Conclusion}

For self-similar search spaces, proof-search difficulty is captured exactly by the
similarity dimension $D = \log s / \log b \in [0,1]$ of the Cantor set of successful
paths. It is simultaneously a fractal exponent, a growth rate, and a relative
entropy; it obeys $s^n = (b^n)^D$ and $(s/b)^n = (b^n)^{D-1}$; it lives on the
balanced edge $[0,1]$ with $D = 1$ a sharp threshold; and operational hardness is the
codimension $1 - D$.

